# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Paco has no application-level authentication
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# There are no accounts, no sign-in, and nothing scoped to a user — every
# chat, session, and setting belongs to the instance as a whole. The only
# access control is an nginx instance password in front of the app, set up
# by the .deb install (docs/self-hosting.md). A development checkout run
# with `pnpm web`, and any container run of this image, have NO nginx in
# front of them and therefore NO password at all: anyone who can reach the
# port reaches the whole app. Neither of those deployment paths must ever be
# exposed to a network — localhost only.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Required
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Postgres connection string. The only one: Drizzle, pg-boss and the durable
# workflow runtime all use it.
#
# Under docker compose the host is the service name, not localhost:
#   postgres://paco:paco@postgres:5432/paco
POSTGRES_URL=

# Derives the key that seals the stored GitHub token. With no accounts and no
# sessions left to sign, this is the only thing APP_SECRET protects — which
# makes it more important to back up, not less. Generate one with:
#   openssl rand -base64 32
#
# Back this up with the database, every time. A dump restored under a
# different APP_SECRET leaves the stored GitHub token permanently
# undecryptable, with no recovery path except reconnecting it from
# Settings -> Connections. See docs/self-hosting.md.
APP_SECRET=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Optional
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The public origin this app is served on, scheme and port included.
#
# A fresh install has no domain yet, and Paco falls back to
# http://localhost:3000 (or http://localhost:$PORT, if PORT is set) when this
# is unset. Set it once you have a real address — pull-request links and the
# port `pnpm dev` listens on are derived from it.
#
# Left commented out on purpose: a compose deploy that just fills in
# POSTGRES_URL and APP_SECRET and runs `docker compose up -d` must not come up
# permanently pinned to a developer's own localhost address. Uncomment it only
# for a value you actually want fixed in the environment; otherwise configure
# the domain from Settings once Paco is running, or set it locally to
# http://localhost:3066 to avoid clashing with the dev servers agents start
# inside sandboxes, which commonly claim :3000 themselves.
# APP_URL=http://localhost:3066

# Host directory holding sandbox workspaces. Defaults to ~/.paco/workspaces.
#
# Set this when running Paco itself in a container: it creates sandboxes as
# sibling containers through the host's Docker socket, so this path has to mean
# the same thing inside Paco and on the host. Bind-mount it at the identical
# path in both. See the repository README.
# PACO_WORKSPACE_ROOT=

# Paco's own data directory — everything that is NOT a sandbox workspace.
# Memory (project and instance scopes) and installed plugins live under it.
#
# Unset, it falls back to the service user's home plus `.paco`: ~/.paco in
# development, /var/lib/paco/.paco under the native package. Nothing migrates
# an existing directory if you change this — memory and plugins both simply
# start looking somewhere new, and the old contents are ignored, not moved.
# PACO_HOME=

# Where plugins are installed, overriding <PACO_HOME>/plugins for plugins only.
#
# Unset, plugins go to <PACO_HOME or ~/.paco>/plugins. A relative value is
# resolved against the server process's working directory, so give it an
# absolute path unless you know exactly what that directory is.
# PACO_PLUGINS_DIR=

# The Node binary plugin workers are spawned with.
#
# Hardened plugin workers require Node >= 24 — PluginHost.start() checks the
# version and refuses below it, because that is the release where the runtime
# gates network sockets in its permission model (see
# packages/plugin-host/SECURITY.md).
#
# Unset, Paco spawns plugin workers with the runtime it is itself running on.
# That is correct under the native package, whose bundled Node satisfies the
# floor. On any host running the server on an older Node, leaving this unset
# means EVERY enabled plugin fails to start: the Plugins page shows them as
# "not-running" and the only diagnosis is a line in the server log reading
# "plugin registry: host runtime is below the required Node floor".
#
# Pointing this somewhere does not bypass the check — start() re-checks
# whatever it resolves to.
# PACO_PLUGIN_NODE_EXECUTABLE=/usr/lib/paco/node/bin/node

# A full sandbox image reference, overriding the tag Paco derives from the
# installed version. For a mirror on a network that cannot reach ghcr.io, or a
# locally built image. Unset, Paco pulls
# ghcr.io/stack256org/paco-sandbox:v<version> on first use.
# PACO_SANDBOX_IMAGE=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Nothing else to configure
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# GitHub  — through the `gh` CLI, which must be on PATH. Connect an account in
#           Settings -> Connections; the token is sealed with APP_SECRET and stored
#           in the database. No GitHub App, no webhook, no credentials here.
# Agent   — the `claude` CLI, which must be on PATH, authenticated once with
#           `claude auth login`. Uses your subscription; no API key. Nothing
#           here needs setting for it.
# Sandbox — `ghcr.io/stack256org/paco-sandbox`, pulled on first use. From a
#           checkout that is the `:latest` tag; an installed host pins
#           `:v<version>` instead, because the container runs as your uid and
#           the image has to be built for that. Override either with
#           PACO_SANDBOX_IMAGE to use a mirror or a local build:
#             docker build -t <your-tag> packages/sandbox/docker
#
# And two workflow variables Paco sets for itself, which an operator must NOT
# set here. An older .env may still carry them; delete them rather than leaving
# a value that is either overwritten or ignored:
#
#   WORKFLOW_POSTGRES_URL — overwritten with POSTGRES_URL by lib/db/migrate.ts,
#                           and ignored at runtime (instrumentation.ts builds
#                           the workflow world from POSTGRES_URL directly).
#   WORKFLOW_TARGET_WORLD — assigned in next.config.ts; always
#                           @workflow/world-postgres.
#
# The same goes for the variables Paco injects into the processes it spawns.
# They are constructed per turn and mean nothing in this file:
#
#   PACO_APPROVAL_URL,    — the approval hook's callback into Paco.
#   PACO_APPROVAL_TOKEN,
#   PACO_APPROVAL_CHAT_ID
#   PACO_INTERNAL_URL,    — the plugin tool bridge's loopback callback, a token
#   PACO_INTERNAL_TOKEN,    scoped to exactly the plugins that bridge fronts,
#   PACO_PLUGIN_TOOLS       and the tool list. The bridge deletes every other
#                           environment key before its first network call, so a
#                           value set here is scrubbed rather than honoured.
#   PACO_PLUGIN_ID,       — the only two variables a plugin worker's
#   PACO_PLUGIN_STATE_DIR   environment carries besides PATH. That environment
#                           is built from scratch, never filtered from this
#                           one, which is why APP_SECRET and POSTGRES_URL are
#                           absent from it.
#   PACO_VERSION          — shipped in the package, pins the sandbox image tag.
#
# And one that is neither: PACO_NODE_EXECUTABLE is read only by
# @paco/plugin-host's test suite, to add a Node binary to the runtime tiers it
# verifies containment on. Nothing in the running app reads it — the variable
# an operator wants is PACO_PLUGIN_NODE_EXECUTABLE, above.
#
# Operating an instance — the instance password, backup and restore, upgrades,
# disk, ports, plugins, memory, tasks, design mode, and schedules:
#   docs/self-hosting.md
# Installing and granting capabilities to a plugin:
#   docs/plugins.md
